    "thinking": "We are in role C with goal \"OpenMP work sharing, reductions, scheduling and thread granularity\". The current source is a sequential sliding-window solution for the maximum sum submatrix problem (Problem B). We need to propose an OpenMP parallelization that demonstrates these concepts. The baseline time is ~0.1346s, best previous own role experiments achieved around 0.059-0.063s with integral image approaches. However, we are not required to use integral image; we can also parallelize the sliding window approach. But note: the sliding window algorithm has a serpentine traversal that updates sum incrementally. Parallelizing it directly is tricky because of data dependencies (the sum update depends on previous state). The typical way to parallelize this problem is either using prefix sums (integral image) or by dividing rows among threads and computing local max then reducing.\n\nGiven the goal \"OpenMP work sharing, reductions, scheduling and thread granularity\", we should demonstrate:\n- Work sharing: #pragma omp for\n- Reductions: reduction(max:mxSum)\n- Scheduling: schedule(static/dynamic/guided) to show control\n- Thread granularity: possibly using collapse or chunk size\n\nThe integral image approach is well-suited: compute prefix sums row-wise and column-wise, then search all top-left corners. That allows easy parallelization of both phases.\n\nPrevious experiments with integral image achieved good scores (0.68-0.69). The best was 0.0596s from experiment c4ed9b25255b4c9f? Actually the interaction list shows a valid outcome with mean_seconds=0.06038671607772509 for source \"c4ed9b25255b4c9f\". That is likely an integral image approach.\n\nWe can propose a similar but slightly different variant: maybe use dynamic scheduling on search phase, or adjust chunk size. But we must be careful not to duplicate exactly previous experiments? The instruction says \"A duplicate has no new reward; its earlier compiler or correctness diagnostic still describe