uling control, we can try a variant that uses schedule(dynamic,1) on the search phase to maximize load balancing at fine granularity, but may increase overhead. Or use schedule(guided) which adapts chunk size.\n\nWe need to produce source code in src/solution.cpp. We'll write an integral image approach with OpenMP parallel for collapse(2) reduction(max:mxSum) and schedule(dynamic). But we must ensure correctness: the prefix sums must be computed correctly, and the submatrix sum formula is correct.\n\nLet's design:\n\n- Read matrix.\n- Compute prefix sum array ps of size (n+1)x(n+1), initialized to 0.\n- First pass: for each row i from 0 to n-1, compute cumulative sum across columns. Use #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static) because rows are independent? Actually the first pass is row-wise prefix sums; we can do it in parallel over rows with static scheduling.\n- Second pass: for each column j from 0 to n-1, accumulate down rows. Again parallel for over columns.\n\nBut careful: The second pass depends on the first pass being complete (all rows done). So we need a barrier between them. We'll use separate parallel regions or just sequential? Better to have two #pragma omp parallel for with implicit barriers.\n\nThen search phase: iterate i from 0 to n-s, j from 0 to n-s. Compute sum = ps[i+s][j+s] - ps[i][j+s] - ps[i+s][j] + ps[i][j]. Use collapse(2) reduction(max:mxSum). We can try schedule(dynamic,1) for fine-grained work sharing.\n\nWe'll also set OMP_NUM_THREADS=4 as per runtime_as_data.\n\nBut we must ensure the code compiles with g++ and OpenMP. Include <omp.h> maybe not needed if using pragmas only? But we need to enable -fopenmp in compilation, but that's handled by Makefile (not our concern). We'll just use #pragma omp parallel for etc.\n\nPotential issue: The matrix size can be up to 8000x8000, so integral image of int may overflow? Sum fits in 32-bit signed integer as per problem statement. But prefix sums could exceed that if we add many numbers; however each element is within 32-bit and N<8000, S<N, but the sum of all elements could be up to N^2 * max_int ~ 64e6*2e9? Actually int can hold up to about 2.1e9, so overflow possible if values are large. But problem guarantees result fits in 32-bit signed integer, but intermediate sums might exceed that? The sliding window algorithm uses incremental updates and also could overflow if not careful. However the original sequential code uses int sum and mxSum; it assumes no overflow because final answer fits. For prefix sums, we need to ensure they don't overflow either. We can use long long for safety, but then performance may degrade. But previous experiments likely used int? The baseline uses int. To be safe, we'll use long long for ps array and cast back to int for mxSum.\n\nBut note: the problem statement says \"It is guaranteed the result will fit a 32-bit signed integer.\" So final answer fits in int. Intermediate sums may exceed if using prefix sums because they accumulate many numbers; but with N<8000, max absolute value of each element? Not specified, could be up to maybe 10^9? Then sum of all elements could be ~64e6*1e9 = 6.4e16 which exceeds int. So we must use long long for prefix sums and submatrix sum calculation, then cast final mxSum to int.\n\nWe'll implement accordingly.\n\nNow, transformations: We are applying OpenMP parallelization with work sharing (parallel for), reductions (max reduction), scheduling control (dynamic schedule on search phase), thread granularity via collapse(2). Also we use static schedule for prefix sums. So transformations list could include \"OpenMP parallel for\", \"reduction(max)\", \"schedule(dynamic,1)\", \"collapse(2)\".\n\nWe need to output JSON with hypothesis, transformations, sources_kept (empty since integration_source_ids is empty), files array containing the new src/solution.cpp content, and notes.\n\nHypothesis: Should describe how we demonstrate OpenMP work sharing, reductions, scheduling, thread granularity. Something like: \"Use integral image computed via two parallel prefix sum passes with static scheduling, then search all top-left corners using collapse(2) reduction(max:mxSum) schedule(dynamic,1). This demonstrates OpenMP work sharing through parallel for directives on both construction and search phases, reductions via max reduction, explicit dynamic scheduling to balance irregular workload in the search phase (due to varying submatrix sums), and thread granularity via collapsed loops.\"\n\nBut we need to ensure it's not identical to previous hypotheses. We can add \"dynamic(1)\" chunk size.\n\nLet's write code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nint main() {\n    int n, s;\n    std::cin >> n >> s;\n    std::vector<std::vector<int>> matrix(n, std::vector<int>(n));\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i)\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j)\n            scanf(\"%d\", &matrix[i][j]);\n\n    // Integral image: prefix sums with extra row/col of zeros\n    std::vector<std::vector<long long>> ps(n + 1, std::vector<long long>(n + 1, 0));\n\n    #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        long long row_sum = 0;\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n            row_sum += matrix[i][j];\n            ps[i + 1][j + 1] = row_sum;\n        }\n    }\n\n    #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\n    for (int j = 0; j <= n; ++j) {\n        long long col_sum = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n            col_sum += ps[i + 1][j];\n            ps[i + 1][j] = col_sum;\n        }\n    }\n\n    int mxSum = 0;\n    #pragma omp parallel for collapse(2) reduction(max:mxSum) schedule(dynamic,1)\n    for (int i = 0; i <= n - s; ++i) {\n        for (int j = 0; j <= n - s; ++j) {\n            long long sum = ps[i + s][j + s] - ps[i][j + s] - ps[i + s][j] + ps[i][j];\n            if (sum > mxSum)\n                mxSum = static_cast<int>(sum);\n        }\n    }\n\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", mxSum);\n}\n```\n\nBut note: The first pass computes row-wise prefix sums but stores only the cumulative sum per cell, not the full 2D prefix. Actually we need to compute ps[i+1][j+1] as sum of matrix[0..i][0..j]. The typical approach is two passes: first accumulate rows (ps[i+1][j+1] = ps[i+1][j] + matrix[i][j]), then columns (ps[i+1][j+1] += ps[i][j+1]). But we can do it in one pass with nested loops? However for parallelization, two passes are easier.\n\nOur first pass: For each row i, compute cumulative sum across j and store in ps[i+1][j+1]. That gives row-wise prefix sums but not column accumulation. Then second pass: For each column j (including 0), accumulate down rows by adding the value from previous row. But careful: after first pass, ps[i+1][j] for j>0 contains sum of matrix[0..i][0..j-1]? Actually we stored only row cumulative sums; so ps[i+1][j+1] = sum_{k=0}^{j} matrix[i][k]. That's not the full 2D prefix. Then second pass: for each column j, iterate i from 0 to n-1 and accumulate: ps[i+1][j] += ps[i][j]; but we need to ensure that after first pass, ps[0][*]=0 already. This will produce correct integral image.\n\nBut note: In the second pass, we are modifying ps in place; it's fine as long as we use the updated values from previous row (which is being written by same thread? Actually if parallelized over columns j, each column is independent because accumulation only depends on previous row of same column. So static schedule works.\n\nHowever, there is a subtlety: after first pass, ps[i+1][j] for j=0 remains 0; we need to accumulate down rows starting from i=0. The second loop should iterate over columns j and then inner loop over i. We'll do:\n\n```cpp\n#pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\nfor (int j = 0; j <= n; ++j) {\n    long long col_sum = ps[0][j]; // which is 0\n    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {\n        col_sum += ps[i][j];\n        ps[i][j] = col_sum;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nBut after first pass, ps[i][j] for j>0 contains row cumulative sum of row i-1 up to column j-1. So this accumulation will produce correct 2D prefix sums.\n\nAlternatively, we can do a simpler approach: compute integral image with two nested loops but parallelize the outer loop over rows and use reduction? That would require synchronization. The above method is fine.\n\nWe must ensure that the search phase uses long long for sum to avoid overflow before casting to int.\n\nAlso note: mxSum initial value should be something like INT_MIN or first submatrix sum. We'll set it to 0 but if all sums negative, max could be negative; problem guarantees result fits in signed int, so we can initialize with a very small number. Better to compute first submatrix and then reduce. But using reduction(max) initial value is implementation-defined? In OpenMP, the private copy of mxSum for each thread will have an undefined initial value if not initialized before parallel region. So we must set mxSum = INT_MIN or something like that. We'll include <climits> and initialize to INT_MIN.\n\nBut note: The reduction(max) in C++ with int works; but the private copies are uninitialized unless we specify a default? Actually, if we have `int mxSum = INT_MIN;` before parallel region, then each thread's private copy will be initialized to that value (the initial value of the shared variable). That is correct. So we'll do:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n...\nint mxSum = INT_MIN;\n#pragma omp parallel for collapse(2) reduction(max:mxSum) schedule(dynamic,1)\nfor (int i = 0; i <= n - s; ++i) {\n    for (int j = 0; j <= n - s; ++j) {\n        long long sum = ps[i + s][j + s] - ps[i][j + s] - ps[i + s][j] + ps[i][j];\n        if (sum > mxSum)\n            mxSum = static_cast<int>(sum);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nBut note: The reduction(max) will automatically update the shared variable with max of private copies. However, we also have an explicit conditional inside; that's fine but redundant because reduction handles it. We can just assign `mxSum = std::max(mxSum, (int)sum);` and re